INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 0, 0, '5. Outline.I.Title and Introduction, 1:1–5.
    II.First Oration: Moses’ Announcement of Deposition, 1:6 to 4:43.
         A.Events from Sinai to Canaan, 1:6 to 3:29.
         B.Admonitions and exhortations to keep the law, 4:1–40.
         C.Appointment of cities of refuge, 4:41–43.
    III.Second Oration: A Review of the Law, 4:44 to 26:19.
         A.Introduction, 4:44–49.
         B.The Decalogue the basis of the covenant, 5:1–33.
         C.Exhortations to obedience, 6:1 to 11:32.
         D.The Book of the Covenant, 12:1 to 26:19.
    IV.Third Oration: The Blessing and the Curse, 27:1 to 28:68.
         A.Introduction, 27:1–13.
         B.The curses, 27:14–26.
         C.Blessing and curses, 28:1–68.
    V.Fourth Oration: The Covenant in Moab, 29:1 to 30:20.
         A.Brief review of events from Egypt to Canaan, 29:1–9.
         B.Exhortation to keep the law, 29:10–29.
         C.Promise of mercy, 30:1–10.
         D.The covenant in the heart, 30:11–20.
    VI.The Responsibilities of Leadership Laid Down, 31:1 to 34:12.
         A.Moses’ appeal to Joshua and to all Israel, 31:1–29.
         B.Moses’ song of victory and parting admonition, 31:30 to 33:29.
         C.Moses’ death, burial, and epitaph, 34:1–12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 1, '1. All Israel. There would seem to be difficulty in the voice of Moses being heard by so large a throng. It is possible, therefore, that his words were repeated by appointed leaders stationed among the people.
This side Jordan. That is, the eastern side of the river. It was “beyond the Jordan” (RSV) as viewed from western Palestine. The region is known today as Transjordan.
The wilderness. The vicinity of the eastern side of Jordan adjacent to the wilderness of wandering.
In the plain. That is, the ’Araba of Moab (Num. 22:1). The term ’Araba, here translated “plain,” applies to all the low-lying country of the river Jordan clear down to the Gulf of Aqaba, or to any part of it. The deepest part of this geographical depression is the Dead Sea.
The Red sea. The word “sea” is not in the Hebrew text. When Moses refers to the Red Sea, he uses the word for “sea” (Deut. 1:40). The Heb. suph, translated “Red,” literally means “reed” (see on Ex. 10:19). Reference here is probably to a locality east of the Jordan not yet identified.
Between Paran. The place names here given do not appear in the account of the Israelite wanderings (Num. 33). Some have identified Hazeroth with the watering place ‘ain Khadra, half way between Sinai and Ezion-geber. Otherwise, nothing is known of the places here mentioned. Laban means “white,” and Dizahab refers to a region “of gold.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 2, '2. The way of mount Seir. That is, by the Mt. Seir road, around the borders of Edom (see on Num. 21:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 3, '3. The forteith year. This included the year in which they left Egypt; the second year, in which they left Sinai after a stay of 11 months; plus 38 years of wandering, until after the death of Aaron (Ex. 19:1; Num. 10:11; Deut. 2:14; see p. 187).
The eleventh month. Two months and nine days before crossing the Jordan (cf. Joshua 4:19). The book of Deuteronomy is a record of what occurred during this interval of time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 4, '4. After he had slain. See Num. 21:21 to 22:1. The two kings here mentioned were conquered in the 40th year of the Exodus. Their defeat was one of the last achievements of Israel under the leadership of Moses.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 5, '5. Began Moses. Literally, “Moses undertook,” or “Moses took upon himself.” The Hebrew verb form here used suggests inward resolution on the part of Moses.
This law. The word here translated “law” is one that refers to instruction generally, and would apply to any and all instruction coming from God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 6, '6. Spake unto us. See Num. 10:13.
In Horeb. See on Ex. 3:1 and 19:1.
Dwelt long enough. Israel encamped at the foot of Mt. Sinai from the third month of the first year of the Exodus (Ex. 19:1) to the 20th day of the second month of the second year (Num. 10:11). Their sojourn at Sinai was devoted to the organization of Israel as a church and a nation, and to the construction of the tabernacle (see on Ex. 3:1; 13:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 7, '7. The mount. Not a particular “mount,” but all the “hill country” (RSV) of Palestine, then Amorite territory. It is the same area as that covered by the 12 spies (Num. 13:17–25).
All the places nigh. “All their neighbors” (RSV). The regions adjacent to the hill country of Palestine are next listed.
The plain. Heb. ‘Arabah, the depression including the lower Jordan valley (see on Deut. 1:1; see also Deut. 3:17; 2 Kings 25:5), the Dead Sea, and the plain extending southward to the Gulf of Aqaba. The Dead Sea was called “the sea of the plain [’Araba]” (Joshua 3:16; 2 Kings 14:25).
The hills. From the same word as “mount” earlier in the verse, in reference to the hilly uplands of central Palestine.
The vale. Heb. Shephelah, a term applied to the foothill country lying between the uplands of central Palestine and the coastal plain, particularly that part of it between Judah and Philistia.
The south. Literally, “the Negeb,” the region about Beersheba and Kadesh-barnea.
The sea side. The coastal plains of Philistia, and that of Sharon, to the north of Philistia.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 8, '8. I have set the land. Literally, “I have given the land,” that is, placed it at your disposal.
Sware unto. A reference to the promises made to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob and recorded in Gen. 15:18; 17:7, 8; 28:13. Compare Gen. 9:9; 17:7-10, 19; 35:12; 48:4; Ex. 28:43; Num. 25:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 9, '9. To bear you. A reference to the advice of Jethro in Ex. 18, and to the appointment of associates to assist Moses in the work of civil administration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 10, '10. Stars of heaven. A reference to the blessing to Abraham (Gen. 22:17) and Jacob (Gen. 26:24). See also Ex. 32:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 11, '11. Hath promised. See Gen. 12:2; 15:5; 17:5, 6; 18:18; 22:17, 18; etc.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 12, '12. Cumbrance. That which wearies one, wears out a person. The word thus translated appears again only in Isa. 1:14, and is there rendered “trouble.” The verb form of the word appears but once (Job 37:11), and is translated “wearieth.”
Burden. A weight to be lifted. The same word is translated “exaction” in Neh. 10:31 and “tribute” in 2 Chron. 17:11.
Strife. Disputes, contentions, controversies (see Gen. 13:7; Deut. 19:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 13, '13. Take you. Literally, “present ye,” or “choose” you (RSV).
Wise. The word thus translated may refer to skill in technical work (Isa. 3:3;Jer. 10:9), wisdom in administration (Gen. 41:33, 39), shrewd men (2 Sam. 13:3), and educated men (Ex. 7:11).
Understanding. From a noun whose root means “to discern,” “to be intelligent,” “to be discreet,” “to have ability to teach.”
Known. That is, “experienced” (see Ex. 18:21, 25). They were recognized to be men of competence and experience.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 14, '14. Ye answered. The verbal acquiescence of the people to the plan of selecting tribal assistants to Moses is here recorded for the first time.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 15, '15. Officers. The word thus translated means “scribe,” or “secretary,” and is from the verb “to write.” The noun probably means “writer” or “secretary,” and refers to one in charge of written records. The same usage of the identical root is found in Arabic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 16, '16. Charged. That is, instructed or gave orders to.
The stranger. A reference to the non-Hebrew who became a proselyte. He was to receive equally fair treatment with the Jew. Uprightness and impartiality in decisions were to mark the professional work of the judges.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 17, '17. Not respect persons. The poor man was to receive equal treatment with the wealthy or with the man of high position. There was to be no respect of persons (see Ex. 23:2, 5; Lev. 19:15).
Judgment is God’s. Riches, power, position, birth, education—none of these were to influence a judicial decision. The judges of Israel stood in the place of God. They were, in fact, called ’elohim, literally “gods,” the very word used of God in such texts as Gen. 1:1–31 (see Ex. 7:1). ’Elohim is applied to judges in Ex. 21:6 and 22:8, 9, in their capacity of speaking for God and giving decisions in His name.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 18, '18. At that time. That is, while at Horeb.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 19, '19. When we departed. Literally, when we “lifted up,” or “pulled up [the stakes and tent pegs].”
Terrible wilderness. Not only vast but hot, subject to great windstorms that whipped up the sand so as to endanger life, and also infested with various poisonous creatures and wild animals.
By the way of. “On the way to” (RSV).
Kadesh-barnea. Where the Israelites remained for a long time in the Wilderness of Paran, after refusing to enter Canaan (Num. 13:3, 26).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 20, '20. Give unto us. The hills of central Palestine were part of the promised inheritance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 21, '21. Fear not. Compare Christ’s use of a similar expression in John 14:27.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 22, '22. The land. See Num. 13:17–20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 23, '23. Twelve men. See Num. 13:1–16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 24, '24. They turned. That is, left Kadesh-barnea behind them and went on their way.
Valley of Eshcol. See Num. 13:24. The word translated “Eshcol” means “cluster,” particularly a cluster of grapes. The valley of Eshcol is thought to have been to the north of Hebron, a region noted for its large clusters of luscious grapes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 25, '25. Fruit. See Num. 13:23.
A good land. See Num. 13:27; 14:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 26, '26. Rebelled. Literally, “were stubbornly rebellious.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 27, '27. Murmured. See Num. 14:1.
Hated us. The children of Israel had become obnoxious to Jehovah because of their idolatry and constant grumbling.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 28, '28. The Anakims. See Num. 13:28, 33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 30, '30. Goeth. That is, in the pillar of cloud by day and of fire by night (see Ex. 13:21; 32:34; Deut. 31:6, 8).
Fight for you. See Ex. 14:14; 17:8; Joshua 10:14, 42; 23:3, 10.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 31, '31. Wilderness. The wilderness of the Red Sea (Ex. 13:18), of Sin (Ex. 16:1), of Sinai (Ex. 19:1, 2), and of Paran (Num. 10:12).
Bare thee. Compare Ex. 19:4; Isa. 46:4; 63:9; Hosea 11:4.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 33, '33. Before you. That is, in the cloud (Ex. 13:21, 22; Num. 14:14).
A place. See Num. 10:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 34, '34. Was wroth. Upon various occasions (Deut. 9:7, 8, 19, 22; Isa. 47:6; 57:16, 17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 35, '35. That good land. So described again and again (Ex. 3:8; Num. 14:7; Deut. 3:25; 4:21; etc.).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 36, '36. Caleb. Together with Joshua, the successor of Moses, Caleb was an exception to the curse incurred by the people at Kadesh (Num. 14:24, 30). Eleazar, son and successor of Aaron, also entered Canaan (Joshua 17:4; 24:33).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 37, '37. Angry with me. Because of insubordination near Kadesh (Num. 20:2–5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 38, '38. Standeth. That is, as an attentive servant. He was given the promise of entrance to Canaan, along with Caleb (Num. 14:6, 7, 30).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 39, '39. Little ones. See Num. 14:31. Their tender age and innocence kept them from being partakers of their parents’ sin.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 41, '41. We have sinned. They pretended to repent and do the Lord’s will—after it was too late, and in fear of the penalty visited upon them (Num. 14:40).
Were ready. Literally, “regarded it a light matter,” from a Hebrew verb meaning “to regard as easy,” “to make light of.” The attitude of the people was therefore somewhat different from the impression given by “were ready.” The RSV reads, “thought it easy.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 42, '42. Go not up. Compare Num. 14:41, 42. The whole attitude of the people reflected shallow thought and superficial repentance. The ark of God’s presence could not accompany them (Num. 14:44).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 43, '43. Went presumptuously. See Num. 14:42–44. The Hebrew verb means “to boil up,” “to seethe,” “to act insolently.” They arrogantly defied the counsel of God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 44, '44. Chased you, as bees do. An apt figure of speech denoting directness, swiftness, and ferocity of attack. There are calculated risks men may take in dealing with the physical world, and yet win, but it is never safe to defy God.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 45, '45. Wept. Their weeping was with tears of mortification and resentment, not of true repentance. “Before the Lord” means at the door of the tabernacle, where they gathered to voice aloud their feelings. Compare the experience noted in Judges 20:23, 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 46, '46. In Kadesh. From the time of their leaving Kadesh-barnea to their arrival at Mt. Hor, about 38 years elapsed. God designed that they should enter immediately. But weeping in perverse impenitence cannot take the place of obedience, nor of genuine repentance.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 1, 0, 'ELLEN G. WHITE COMMENTS
  1-46 TM 420
  2 PP 376, 387
  15 Ed 37; PP 374
  16, 17 AA 94; PP 381
  22 PP 387
  41 PP 392
  45 PP 394');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 0, 'Palestine in Biblical Times');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 1, '1. We turned. They now went southward, toward the shore of the Red Sea.
Mount Seir. The hilly country of the Edomites, of which Mt. Seir itself was part (see 1 Kings 9:26; 2 Chron. 8:17).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 3, '3. Long enough. Thirty-nine years had passed since the Israelites’ departure from Egypt; they spent about 38 years in wandering.
Northward. That is, from Ezion-geber toward Moab and Canaan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 4, '4. Your brethren. The descendants of Esau (Deut. 23:7; Amos 1:11; Obadiah 10, 12; Mal. 1:2).
Take ye good heed. The inhabitants of Edom would be nervous and apprehensive, and might as a result make a sudden attack upon Israel. On the other hand, their awareness of the fear in the hearts of the Edomites might lead Israel to take advantage of that fear and invade the country.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 5, '5. Unto Esau. See Joshua 24:4. God deals faithfully even with those outside the covenant relationship. God had promised certain temporal blessings to the descendants of Esau, and would fulfill His word.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 6, '6. Buy meat. Literally, “buy food” (see Gen. 41:35, 36, 48; 42:7, 10; 44:1, 25; 47:24). The verb translated “to buy” is of buying grain (Gen. 47:14), whereas the noun from the same root means “corn” (Gen. 42:1, 2, 19, 26; 43:2; 44:2; 47:14; Amos 8:5).
Buy water. Water is a precious commodity in the desert wastes, and may easily become the cause of strife (see Gen. 26:17–22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 7, '7. Thy walking. The care of God applies even to the details of the journey of life (Ps. 1:6).
Forty years. A round number. From the Passover in Egypt to the first Passover in Canaan, at Gilgal (Joshua 4:19; 5:10), was exactly 40 years (see p. 187).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 8, '8. Elath. Their direction from Seir had been southward to Ezion-geber on the Gulf of Aqaba and Elath, which probably lay close to Ezion-geber on the coast. Then skirting the territory of Edom as they turned and traveled northward, they reached the territory of Moab.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 9, '9. Given Ar. The descendants of Lot, like those of Ishmael and Esau, had their inheritance confirmed to them before the seed of Abraham occupied what had been assigned to them. Ar was apparently the chief city of that territory, and gave its name to the entire region (Num. 21:15, 28).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 10, '10. Emims. For mention of the people and their city, Shaveh Kiriathaim, see Gen. 14:5 and Joshua 13:19.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 12, '12. Horims. The ancient inhabitants of Mt. Seir (Gen. 14:6; 36:20), the Hurrians, whose history, language, and religion have but recently come to light (see p. 138).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 13, '13. The brook Zered. Compare Num. 21:12. The modern Wâdi el–Hesa, at the southeastern corner of the Dead Sea.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 14, '14. The men of war. An expression used to designate men 20 years of age and over (Num. 1:3).
Wasted out. Not one remained (Num. 26:64, 65).
The Lord sware. See Num. 14:28, 29.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 18, '18. Pass over. That is, to skirt the border, but without crossing it.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 19, '19. Children of Ammon. They were not to trespass upon the territory of the children of Ammon, but to pass through the territory of Sihon the king in Heshbon (see Num. 21:13, 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 20, '20. Zamzummins. See Gen. 14:5. Beyond the fact that these people were a race of giant stature, and were driven out of their country by the Ammonites, we have no information concerning them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 21, '21. Destroyed them. The Ammonites were used by the Lord to discipline the people.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 22, '22. The Horims. See v. 12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 23, '23. Avims. See Joshua 13:3, 4. The original inhabitants of southwest Palestine, dispossessed by the Philistines.
Caphtorims. See on Gen. 10:14; also Jer. 47:4; Amos 9:7.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 24, '24. Rise ye up. See Num. 21:13. The command to cross the Arnon and invade the territory adjacent to the Jordan inhabited by the Amorites.
Sihon the Amorite. Sihon had taken this territory from the Moabites, who in turn withdrew to the south of the Arnon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 25, '25. The dread. Compare the promise to Moses (Ex. 15:15, 16), and the experience of Joshua with the Amorites at Gibeon (Joshua 10:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 26, '26. Kedemoth. See Joshua 13:18; 21:37; 1 Chron. 6:79, where a city of that name is mentioned in the plain of Jordan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 27, '27. High way. A royal highway maintained for the expeditious travel of officials and troops (Num. 20:17; 21:22). Traces of this ancient highway remain today, from the Gulf of Aqaba northward through Transjordan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 28, '28. Thou shalt sell. The Israelites offered every inducement for the granting of a peaceful passage.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 29, '29. Pass over Jordan. An assurance to Sihon that they had no intention of settling in his land, since their inheritance lay beyond the Jordan.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 30, '30. Not let us pass by. He was suspicious of their sincerity and questioned the reasonableness of their request (see Num. 21:23).
Hardened his spirit. That is, allowed Sihon’s obstinacy to run its course.
Made his heart obstinate. The Hebrew verb thus translated means “to be strong,” “to be stout,” “to be firm.” It is elsewhere translated “strengthen” (Deut. 3:28; Ps. 27:14), “confirm” (Isa. 35:3), “fortify” (Nahum 2:1), “was stedfastly minded” (Ruth 1:18). The Lord would not interfere with the natural operation of Sihon’s heart and intents, but would confirm him in it (see on Ex. 4:21). One may use the God-given strength of mind and heart to continue in the wrong or to turn to the right. The Lord does not force a man to the one or to the other, but ever stands ready to cooperate by His Spirit with the man who chooses the good. The same word is translated in Joshua 1:6 as “be … of a good courage.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 34, '34. Utterly destroyed. Literally, “placed under a ban,” “set apart.” Such an act might be performed only at God’s command. The same was done with respect to Jericho.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 36, '36. Aroer. An Amorite city on the right bank of the river Arnon, about 13 mi. from the Dead Sea (see Joshua 12:2; 13:16; 2 Kings 10:33). Its modern name is ‘Arâ ‘ir.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 37, '37. Jabbok. See Num. 21:24; Judges 11:22. According to the divine command the territory of the Ammonites was not invaded. Ambition must ever be in harmony with the will of God and not seek to go beyond the limits He has set. Then success in this life will be all blessing. But if one goes beyond God’s will to seek more than He permits, success in the venture may well prove to be Dead Sea fruit.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 2, 0, 'ELLEN G. WHITE COMMENTS
  1-37 PP 433-435
  3-6 PP 414
  4, 5 PP 423
  6, 7 PP 424
  7 PP 406
  9 PP 433
  14, 15 PP 406
  19 PP 433
  24, 25 PP 434
  27, 28, 30 PP 433
  32-34 PP 435');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 1, '1. Turned, and went. See Num. 21:32, 33.
Bashan. Probably from the Hebrew root meaning “to be soft,” “to be smooth.” The noun as a proper name would mean “smooth,” “fertile,” which is true of the region from the Yarmuk northward to Hermon. It has few trees, but a rich soil ideal for grain.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 2, '2. Fear him not. A repetition of Num. 21:34.
Deliver him. Compare Ex. 23:31; Deut. 7:24; 20:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 3, '3. Smote him. See Num. 21:35.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 4, '4. Argob. See 1 Kings 4:13. The meaning of the Heb. word ’Argob is not known.The area is east of the Sea of Galilee in the highland region of southeast Bashan, and included the cities of Karnaim and Ashtaroth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 6, '6. Destroyed. The same word as in ch. 2:34 and meaning, basically, “to devote to,” “to dedicate,” that is, here, to destruction.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 8, '8. The land. The territory allotted to Gad and Reuben and the half tribe of Manasseh.
Arnon unto mount Hermon. The southern and northern boundaries of “the land.” Hermon, some 9,100 ft. above sea level, dominates the entire region.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 9, '9. Sirion. As the borders of several nations met at Mt. Hermon, it was given a name by each border people in their own language. The name Sirion, for Hermon, appears in the Ugaritic literature of northern Syria. For other instances of such differences in names see Gen. 23:2 and 31:47.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 10, '10. All the cities. The various types of territory within the conquered country are here mentioned.
Salchah and Edrei. See Joshua 13:11; Num. 21:33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 11, '11. Giants. Literally, “Rephaim,” a very ancient people included among those defeated by Chedorlaomer and the kings associated with him (Gen. 14:5). They lived on both sides of the Jordan, and seem to have belonged to an earlier group of inhabitants of the region.
Bedstead. The word thus translated is also given as “couch” (Ps. 6:6; Amos 3:12; 6:4). It may refer also to a sarcophagus, or tomb.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 13, '13. Land of giants. Literally, the “land of the Rephaim” (see on v. 11). King Og was the last of the “giants.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 14, '14. Jair. His mother was of the tribe of Manasseh, but his father was of the tribe of Judah (see 1 Chron. 2:22). Jair had conquered this territory, which was, accordingly, given to him and his descendants (Num. 32:41).
Geshuri and Maachathi. This was the district between the Yarmuk and Hermon, being the western part of Bashan (see Gen. 22:24; 2 Sam. 15:8; 1 Chron. 19:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 15, '15. Machir. That portion of Gilead that had not been given to the children of Gad was allotted to the descendants of Machir (Num. 32:40).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 16, '16. Half the valley. The stream that flowed through the valley, or wadi, was to form the boundary.
Jabbok. This stream was to be the other boundary of the territory. It was here that Jacob wrestled with the Angel (Gen. 32:22–24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 17, '17. Chinnereth. The Sea of Chinnereth (Joshua 12:3; 13:27). There was also a city of the same name (Joshua 19:35), from which the lake received its name. It was on the northwestern shore of the lake, its ruins now being known as Tell el–‘Oreimeh. The Sea of Chinnereth is the NT Sea of Galilee, also called the Lake of Gennesaret, and later known as the Sea of Tiberias.
The salt sea. Known also as the Dead Sea, “the sea of the plain” (Deut. 4:49; 2 Kings 14:25, cf. Gen. 14:3; Num. 34:3, 12).
Under Ashdoth-pisgah eastward. Literally, “under the slopes of Pisgah on the east” (RSV). Mt. Pisgah, with its peak, Nebo, now called Râs es–Siâghah, is in the mountains of Abarim, overlooking the Dead Sea from the eastern side (see Num. 27:12; Deut. 34:1–3).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 18, '18. I commanded you. A reference to the command given to the tribes of Reuben and Gad and the half tribe of Manasseh (see Num. 32:20).
Hath given you this land. As they had specifically requested (Num. 32:20–22).
Armed. See Num. 32:17–32.
Before your brethren. The members of the two tribes were to be an advance guard of the army of Israel. Compare the valor of Gad in ch. 33:20.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 19, '19. Your wives. See Num. 32:16, 24, 26.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 20, '20. Rest. When the Promised Land was occupied, Joshua dismissed the armies of the two and a half tribes to return to their own territory on the other side of Jordan (Joshua 22:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 21, '21. Thine eyes have seen all. Literally, “thine own eyes, these were the ones seeing all.” Moses’ appeal was to the people to remember the way by which God had led them.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 22, '22. The Lord. The last half of the verse reads literally, “Jehovah your God, He is the one fighting for you.” The pronoun “he” is emphatic.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 23, '23. I besought. The form of the Hebrew verb suggests the translation, “I was seeking favor of the Lord for myself.” This was when God told Moses that he would not enter the Land of Promise but must die at the border (Num. 27:12, 13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 24, '24. Begun to shew. Moses was eager to witness the end of the conquest of Canaan, as he had its beginning.
Mighty hand. See Joshua 4:24. The hand is a symbol of power, for it is the instrument by which power is exercised. It is translated “dominion” in 1 Chron. 18:3 and 2 Chron. 21:8.
What God is there? Moses knew that other nations believed in a heaven peopled by many goods, but he knew that there was but one God; all others were figments of the imagination.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 25, '25. See. In the sense of becoming acquainted with firsthand, that is, by personal experience.
That goodly mountain, and Lebanon. Literally, “this good hill country and Lebanon,” or perhaps better, “this good mountain, even Lebanon.” The name Lebanon, from the verb “to be white,” means “white (mountain).” The cool, wooded slopes of the mountain and its snow-covered cap looked inviting and desirable in contrast to the desert country. Moses longed to walk in the cool of Lebanon.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 26, '26. Was wroth. From a word whose root means “to pass over.” The form here used being reflexive, the word means “to exceed the limit,” that is, to be beside oneself in respect to someone or some incident (see Ps. 78:21, 59, 62).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 27, '27. Pisgah. See on v. 17.
Not go over. Compare Deut. 31:2 and Joshua 1:2, 11.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 28, '28. He shall go over. The pronoun is emphatic: “He is the one who shall cross over.” By this emphasis Moses knew that the decision was irrevocable.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 29, '29. Beth-peor. The first word of this compound term means “house.” The second word, “peor,” may be from a root meaning “to be greedy,” “to be open,” “to have eager desire.” In this case the name would mean, “the house of desire.” In this vicinity Moses was buried by God (ch. 34:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 3, 0, 'ELLEN G. WHITE COMMENTS
  1-11 PP 435-437
  2 PP 436
  4, 5 PP 435
  24-27 PP 462
  25 DA 421; PP 479
  26 PP 419, 420; 3T 319
  26, 27 SR 166
  27 MH 508');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 1, '1. Hearken. In the sense of “give heed.” The third chapter concluded the review of the journey of Israel from Horeb to the river Jordan, preliminary to crossing it. Chapter 4 consists largely of an exhortation to obedience.
Statutes. That is, the provisions of the civil code regulating conduct, in contrast to the “judgments,” which were concerned with the administration of the “statutes,” particularly with respect to judicia decisions. The word rendered “judgments” is sometimes also translated “right,” in the sense of “justice,” or a “right decision” (Gen. 18:25; Job 34:6, 7; 35:2; Ps. 9:4).
Ye may live. Their fathers had died in the wilderness because of disobedience; they were to live—if they obeyed God—in the land promised their fathers.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 2, '2. Ye shall not add. Whatever God does is perfect. To add to it or to subtract from it is to mar or spoil it (Deut. 12:32; Jer. 26:2; Rev. 22:18).
Commandments. From the Hebrew commonly translated “command” or “commandment” (Gen. 26:5; Ex. 15:26; Lev. 4:2; Num. 15:22; Ps. 78:7; etc.).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 3, '3. Because of Baal-peor. Literally, “in Baal-peor.” For the sin and its results at this place see Num. 25:1–5; Deut. 3:29; Hosea 9:10. Some scholars think that the Baal-peor here mentioned was a god of sexual desire, such as is worshipped so freely by the Lingayats in India today.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 4, '4. Cleave. This word stands for the closest possible relationship, like that of husband and wife (Gen. 2:24; see also Job 19:20 and Jer. 13:11).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 6, '6. Keep therefore and do. To “keep”them is to give assent of mind and heart to them, with intent to order the life accordingly; to “do” them is to carry out the intent of the will. A man must purpose to do right before he does right. These two exhortations are repeated again and again (chs. 7:12; 16:12; 23:23; 24:8; 26:16; 28:13). It is the practical carrying out of God’s will that He appreciates and values highly.
Your wisdom. The respect of Israel among the nations would be in proportion to their faithfulness in observing the commandments of God. The blessings of God upon His people as they lived in harmony with His requirements would greatly impress the surrounding nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 8, '8. All this law. Literally, “all this torah.” The word torah is inclusive of all instruction and doctrine, and comprehends all principles of conduct. Moses suggests the idea of comparing God’s “law,” or principles of conduct, with those of the surrounding nations.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 9, '9. Diligently. Constant vigilance is necessary in order that the spiritual life may ever be in tune with God’s revealed will.
Teach them thy sons. Literally, “make them known to thy children” (see Ex. 12:26; 13:8, 14; Joshua 4:21).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 10, '10. In Horeb.  The memorable occasion when God revealed His holy law. The memory of this occasion was ever to be kept clearly in mind.
Fear me. To “fear” God is to regard Him with profound and reverent respect (Ex. 19:10–13; 20:20) and to have proper regard for His will (Deut. 8:6; Prov. 3:7; Eccl. 12:13; Isa. 11:2, 3; 33:6).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 11, '11. Ye came near. See Ex. 19:17.
Burned. That is, the mountain had the appearance of being on fire, like the “burning” bush (Ex. 3:2; cf. Heb. 12:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 12, '12. The Lord spake. See Ex. 19:20; 20:1, 22.
Midst of the fire. See Ex. 19:18; 24:17; Deut. 4:15, 33, 36; 5:4, 22, 24; 9:10; 10:4.
The voice. Literally, “the sound,” that is, of the words God spoke (see Ex. 19:19; 24:16).
Saw no similitude. Literally, “saw no form.” “Similitude” is rendered “likeness” in Deut. 4:23, 25; 5:8; Ps. 17:15, and “form” in Job 4:16.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 13, '13. Covenant. The Hebrew word thus translated may be applied to any agreement or contract. It probably comes from the Hebrew verb “to bind,” “to fetter.” It is used of a treaty or alliance (Gen. 14:13; Ex. 23:32), an agreement (2 Sam. 3:12, 13, 21; Jer. 34:8), and of the marriage vow (Mal. 2:14).
Ten commandments. Literally, “the ten words.” These were later written by God Himself (Ex. 24:12; 34:28; Deut. 10:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 14, '14. Commanded me. God Himself presented the Ten Commandments, but all the civil and ceremonial laws were given through Moses (Ex. 24:3). This important distinction Moses repeatedly emphasizes (Ex. 20:1, 19; 21:1; 24:3; Deut. 5:22). The book of Deuteronomy is concerned primarily with the civil laws.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 15, '15. Similitude. Literally, “form” (see on v. 12). Man is prone to seek to express his concepts of deity in visible, material form. The manifestation of divine glory at Sinai was not to be an excuse for doing so.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 16, '16. Corrupt yourselves. See Ex. 20:4. Heathen nations have degraded themselves by depicting deity in forms partly human and partly animal and by making grotesque representations of the human form.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 18, '18. Creepeth. The worship of snakes and other lowly creatures is common among millions of people even today.
Fish. The ancient Philistines. Mesopotamians, and possibly the Egyptians included in their pantheon a fish god (see Ex. 20:4).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 19, '19. The host of heaven. The worship of the heavenly bodies is a snare into which man has fallen from earliest times. Today such worship is common in many Eastern countries. For a record of this sin among God’s people see 2 Kings 17:16; 21:3, 5; 23:4, 5; Jer. 44:18, 19; Eze. 8:16; Zeph. 1:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 20, '20. The iron furnace. In 1 Kings 8:51 and Jer. 11:4 this figure of speech is used to denote harsh servitude. Isaiah defines the disciplinary experiences under God’s hand as a process of refining in a furnace (Isa. 48:10; see also Job 23:10).
People of inheritance. Or, “his own inheritance” (see ch. 32:9).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 21, '21. Lord was angry. For the third time Moses speaks of this fact, in an endeavor to impress upon Israel the liabilities involved in opposition to God’s will (ch. 1:37; 3:26).
Giveth. Literally, “on the point of giving,” or “about to give” (see also chs. 15:4; 25:19; 26:1).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 22, '22. I must die. Moses felt keenly his being forbidden entrance to the land of Promise (see Num. 27:12–14).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 23, '23. A graven image. The danger of idolatrous practices lay heavily on the heart of Moses (see vs. 16, 25).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 24, '24. A consuming fire. Compare Ps. 50:3; Isa. 29:6; 30:27, 30; Amos 5:6; Zeph. 1:18; Heb. 12:29.
A jealous God. God will not tolerate divided. affections or halfhearted service (Ex. 20:5).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 25, '25. Remained long. Literally, “fallen asleep.” The Hebrew word thus used means “to sleep,” and may be used figuratively of the dulling spiritual life, or the loss of one’s first impressions.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 26, '26. Not prolong. They would perish as a nation (see Deut. 5:33; 11:9; 17:20; 22:7; 30:18; 32:47).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 27, '27. Scatter you. See Lev. 26:32, 33.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 28, '28. Serve gods. This verse is descriptive of the most debasing form of idolatry (see 2 Kings 19:18), (Ps. 115:4; 135:15; Micah 5:13).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 29, '29. All thy heart. One’s motives must be pure and spiritual (see chs. 6:5; 10:12; 11:13; 30:2, 6, 10).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 30, '30. Latter days. Literally, “in the afterdays.” This expression is frequent in a prophetic sense, pointing to the Messiah and to His second coming and kingdom (see Isa. 2:2; Hosea 3:5; Acts 2:17; Heb. 1:1, 2; 1 Peter 1:20; 1 John 2:18).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 31, '31. Merciful. The root from which the adjective thus translated comes means “to love,” “to be inclined toward affection,” and in the intensive form, “to have compassion.” In the 13 times this adjective is used in the OT, it is always applied to God.
He will not forsake thee. Literally, “He will not let thee sink down.” The verb is often translated, “to become feeble,” “to become weakened,” “to faint.”');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 32, '32. For ask now. It strengthens the believer to recall God’s dealings in times past (see LS 196).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 33, '33. People hear. This reflects their awe in the presence of God. Sinful man cannot literally see God and live (Ex. 33:20; Judges 13:22).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 34, '34. Assayed. That is, “ventured” or “attempted.”
Temptations. Literally, “trials.” There is no thought here of leading into sin but rather of trying experiences.
Signs. See Ex. 4:9; 7:9, 10.
Wonders. A reference to the plagues of Egypt (Ex. 7:3; 11:9, 10).
War. The defeat of the Egyptians at the Red Sea (Ex. 14:14; 15:3).
A mighty hand. See Deut. 3:24; 9:29; also Ex. 6:6; 7:5.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 36, '36. His great fire. A reference to the glory of God (see on Deut. 4:11, 24).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 37, '37. He chose their seed. See Gen. 12:7; 13:15, 16; 22:17, 18; Ex. 32:13.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 38, '38. Drive out. Literally, “dispossessed,” as it is translated in Num. 32:39.
Greater and mightier. So much so that at the report of the spies Israel became fearful and rebelled against God (Num. 13:28–31).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 39, '39. None else. See Isa. 44:8; Acts 4:12.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 40, '40. Prolong thy days. See chs. 5:16; 6:2; 11:9. Here closes Moses’ first oration.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 41, '41. Severed three cities. Literally, “caused three cities to be separated,” that is, the cities of refuge. The same word, translated “separated,” is used of the setting apart of the tribe of Levi to carry the sacred furniture of the tabernacle (ch. 10:8). Ezekiel 42:20 speaks of a “separation between the sanctuary and the profane place.”
Sun rising. That is, “to the east,” meaning east of the Jordan, in the territory occupied by the two and a half tribes.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 42, '42. These cities. See ch. 19:1–13 for the laws respecting the cities of refuge.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 43, '43. Bezer. From a word meaning “fortress” or “enclosure.” The word is translated “defence” in Job 22:25. The site is possibly Umm el–Ammad.
Ramoth. The word thus translated is from a root meaning “coral.” Ramoth played an important role in the later history of the kings of Israel (see 1 Kings 4:7, 13; 22:1–40; 2 Kings 8:28, 29). Now called Tell Rāmîth, this site lies 25 mi. east of the Jordan as the crow flies.
Golan. Probably from a word meaning “circle.” It is usually identified with the modern village Sahem el–Jōlân, 18 mi. east of the Sea of Chinnereth.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 44, '44. The law. The torah, meaning God’s instructions in general, inclusive of the Ten Commandments.
Set before. Literally, “to place,” “to lay,” here with the idea of placing the law before their eyes (see Ex. 19:7, where it is rendered “laid”).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 45, '45. The testimonies. The Hebrew word thus translated is used 60 times in the OT, almost without exception of the Ten Commandments.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 46, '46. Smote. In the 40th year of the Exodus (Num. 21:24; 33:38; see Israel’s Campaign East of the Jordan).');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 47, '47. On this side Jordan. See Joshua 1:15; 12:1.');
INSERT INTO commentaries (author, book, chapter, verse, text) VALUES ('Comentário Bíblico Adventista I', 'Deuteronômio', 4, 48, '48. Mount Sion. Not Mt. Zion in Jerusalem, but either another name for Sirion, the Sidonian name for Mt. Hermon, or the name of one of its peaks (Deut. 3:8, 9, 12; Ps. 29:6).');
